<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alpes Graiae</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alpes Graiae was a small province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, covering the Tarentaise valley in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savoie">Savoie</a> and the approaches to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-st-bernard-pass">Little St Bernard Pass</a>. Organised in the 1st century CE, probably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, it was governed by an equestrian procurator from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aime-savoie">Forum Claudii Ceutronum</a> (Aime), and was later administered jointly with the neighbouring Alpes Poeninae.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alpes-graiae.jpg" alt="Alpes Graiae" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Alpes_Poenninae_et_Graiae_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The province of the Alpes Graiae et Poeninae joined two Alpine districts, the Alpes Poeninae (also called Vallis Poenina) and the Alpes Graiae (also called Alpes Atrectianae). It comprised the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, the Tarantaise Valley, Beaufortain and Haut-Faucigny in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta-valley">Aosta Valley</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It was one of three provinces straddling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> between modern France and Italy, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alpes-maritimae">Alpes Maritimae</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cottian-alps">Alpes Cottiae</a>.</p><p>The names are of local origin. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, Poeninae comes from an indigenous deity, Poeninus, attested on inscriptions and in the cult title Jupiter Poeninus; the name survives in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennine-alps">Pennine Alps</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a>. Xavier Delamarre proposed a Celtic god *Graios as the source of Graiae, while Guy Barruol suggested that Atrectianae may come from an unattested local king, *Atrectius, by analogy with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cottian-alps">Alpes Cottiae</a>, though no direct evidence for such a ruler exists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the time of the Roman conquest the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a> was inhabited by Celtic tribes known as the Vallenses: the Nantuates, Veragri, Seduni and Uberi. After the Roman invasion led by Augustus in 16–15 BCE the area was placed under military control, under a praefectus Raetis, Vindolicis, vallis Poeninae, and incorporated into the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a> et Vindelicia, which stretched between the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> (41–54 CE) the Vallensian tribes received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> rights and were grouped into a single civitas Vallensium. Most scholars place the start of the administrative reorganisation that linked the Vallis Poenina to the Alpes Graiae, the Ceutron territory west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-st-bernard-pass">Little St Bernard Pass</a>, in this period. An inscription of his reign still shows the valley under the procurator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>, however, and some scholars date the definitive union to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> (193–211) instead.</p><p>By the 2nd century the combined province had a procurator seated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aime-savoie">Axima</a>, which became Forum Claudii Ceutronum (modern Aime-la-Plagne), the chief town of the Graian division. He was assisted by a praefectus at Octodurus, which became Forum Claudii Vallensium (Martigny), the centre of the Poenine district. An inscription of 193–235 uses the variant Alpium Atrectianarum et Vallis Poeninae.</p><p>Under the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> (284–305) the province was placed under a praeses in the Dioecesis Galliarum. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the region, Theodul, is mentioned in 381. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> the region was invaded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> and joined their kingdom. It passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish Kingdom</a> in 534 and was briefly invaded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> in 574.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alpes Graiae et Poeninae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their town Axima (Aime) became Forum Claudii Ceutronum, capital of the small province of <b>Alpes Graiae</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ceutrones">Ceutrones</a></li></ul></section>
